{"notes":[{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.01700v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"wadhawan|vapr_visionlanguage_preference_alignment_for_reasoning"},"authorids":{"value":["~Rohan_Wadhawan1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Fabrice_Harel-Canada:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zi-Yi_Dou:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Suhaila_Shakiah:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Robinson_Piramuthu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Nanyun_Peng_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.01700"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2510-01700,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Rohan Wadhawan and Fabrice Harel-Canada and Zi-Yi Dou and Suhaila Shakiah and Robinson Piramuthu and Nanyun Peng},\n  title={VaPR - Vision-language Preference alignment for Reasoning},\n  year={2025},\n  month={October},\n  cdate={1759276800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2510.01700},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.01700}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Preference finetuning methods like Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) with AI-generated feedback have shown promise in aligning Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) with human preferences. However, existing techniques overlook the prevalence of noise in synthetic preference annotations in the form of stylistic and length biases. To this end, we introduce a hard-negative response generation framework based on LLM-guided response editing, that produces rejected responses with targeted errors, maintaining stylistic and length similarity to the accepted ones. Using this framework, we develop the VaPR dataset, comprising 30K high-quality samples, to finetune three LVLM families: LLaVA-V1.5, Qwen2VL & Qwen2.5VL (2B-13B sizes). Our VaPR models deliver significant performance improvements across ten benchmarks, achieving average gains of 6.5% (LLaVA), 4.0% (Qwen2VL), and 1.5% (Qwen2.5VL), with notable improvements on reasoning tasks. A scaling analysis shows that performance consistently improves with data size, with LLaVA models benefiting even at smaller scales. Moreover, VaPR reduces the tendency to answer \"Yes\" in binary questions - addressing a common failure mode in LVLMs like LLaVA. Lastly, we show that the framework generalizes to open-source LLMs as editors, with models trained on VaPR-OS achieving ~99% of the performance of models trained on \\name, which is synthesized using GPT-4o. Our data, models, and code can be found on the project page https://vap-r.github.io"},"title":{"value":"VaPR - Vision-language Preference alignment for Reasoning"},"authors":{"value":["Rohan Wadhawan","Fabrice Harel-Canada","Zi-Yi Dou","Suhaila Shakiah","Robinson Piramuthu","Nanyun Peng"]}},"tmdate":1770226539224,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2510-01700"],"tcdate":1770226531292,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Rohan_Wadhawan1"],"forum":"K4Tp2h2dDD","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":819278,"cdate":1759276800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1770226539224,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"K4Tp2h2dDD","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"VaPR, a hard-negative preference dataset that mitigates stylistic and length biases in AI feedback, enabling improved reasoning and robustness in preference finetuned (DPO) vision-language models across ten benchmarks."},"venue":{"value":"COLM 2025"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the COLM Code of Ethics on https://colmweb.org/CoE.html"},"keywords":{"value":["Vision Language Models","Preference Optimization","DPO","Data Generation","Reasoning"]},"abstract":{"value":"Preference finetuning methods like Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) with AI-generated feedback have shown promise in aligning Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) with human preferences. However, existing techniques overlook the prevalence of noise in synthetic preference annotations in the form of stylistic and length biases. To this end, we introduce a hard-negative response generation framework based on LLM-guided response editing, that produces rejected responses with targeted errors, maintaining stylistic and length similarity to the accepted ones. Using this framework, we develop the VaPR dataset, comprising 30K high-quality samples, to finetune three LVLM families: LLaVA-V1.5, Qwen2VL \\& Qwen2.5VL (2B-13B sizes). Our VaPR models deliver significant performance improvements across ten benchmarks, achieving average gains of 6.5% (LLaVA), 4.0% (Qwen2VL), and 1.5% (Qwen2.5VL), with notable improvements on reasoning tasks. A scaling analysis shows that performance consistently improves with data size, with LLaVA models benefiting even at smaller scales. Moreover, VaPR reduces the tendency to answer \"Yes\" in binary questions - addressing a common failure mode in LVLMs like LLaVA. Lastly, we show that the framework generalizes to open-source LLMs as editors, with models trained on VaPR-OS achieving ~99% of the performance of models trained on VaPR, which is synthesized using GPT-4o. Our data, models, and code can be found on the project page https://vap-r.github.io/vap-r/"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nwadhawan2025vapr,\ntitle={Va{PR} - Vision-language Preference alignment for Reasoning},\nauthor={Rohan Wadhawan and Fabrice Y Harel-Canada and Zi-Yi Dou and Suhaila Shakiah and Robinson Piramuthu and Nanyun Peng},\nbooktitle={Second Conference on Language Modeling},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=uBAubFwymy}\n}"},"title":{"value":"VaPR - Vision-language Preference alignment for Reasoning"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/70b7995114502f1826a2a3c5a3f55008f8261306.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"colmweb.org/COLM/2025/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"wadhawan|vapr_visionlanguage_preference_alignment_for_reasoning"},"authorids":{"value":["~Rohan_Wadhawan1","~Fabrice_Y_Harel-Canada1","~Zi-Yi_Dou1","~Suhaila_Shakiah1","~Robinson_Piramuthu1","~Nanyun_Peng1"]},"author_guide":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://colmweb.org/AuthorGuide.html"},"authors":{"value":["Rohan Wadhawan","Fabrice Y Harel-Canada","Zi-Yi Dou","Suhaila Shakiah","Robinson Piramuthu","Nanyun Peng"]}},"tmdate":1756169212349,"pdate":1751944443015,"tcdate":1742375598298,"writers":["colmweb.org/COLM/2025/Conference","colmweb.org/COLM/2025/Conference/Submission403/Authors"],"signatures":["colmweb.org/COLM/2025/Conference/Submission403/Authors"],"forum":"uBAubFwymy","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":403,"cdate":1742375598298,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["colmweb.org/COLM/2025/Conference/-/Submission","colmweb.org/COLM/2025/Conference/-/Post_Submission","colmweb.org/COLM/2025/Conference/Submission403/-/Full_Submission","colmweb.org/COLM/2025/Conference/-/Edit","colmweb.org/COLM/2025/Conference/Submission403/-/Camera_Ready"],"mdate":1756169212349,"odate":1756169212332,"domain":"colmweb.org/COLM/2025/Conference","id":"uBAubFwymy","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"PPoPP 2022"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/PPOPP/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"zheng|vapro_performance_variance_detection_and_diagnosis_for_productionrun_parallel_applications"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Liyan_Zheng_0001:","~Jidong_Zhai1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiongchao_Tang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Haojie_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Teng_Yu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuyang_Jin_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shuaiwen_Leon_Song:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wenguang_Chen:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3503221.3508411"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/ppopp/ZhengZTWYJSC22,\n  author={Liyan Zheng and Jidong Zhai and Xiongchao Tang and Haojie Wang and Teng Yu and Yuyang Jin and Shuaiwen Leon Song and Wenguang Chen},\n  title={Vapro: performance variance detection and diagnosis for production-run parallel applications},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  pages={150-162},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/3503221.3508411},\n  booktitle={PPoPP},\n  crossref={conf/ppopp/2022}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Performance variance is a serious problem for parallel applications, which can cause performance degradation and make applications' behavior hard to understand. Therefore, detecting and diagnosing performance variance are of crucial importance for users and application developers. However, previous detection approaches either bring too large overhead and hurt applications' performance, or rely on nontrivial source code analysis that is impractical for production-run parallel applications.In this work, we propose Vapro, a performance variance detection and diagnosis framework for production-run parallel applications. Our approach is based on an important observation that most parallel applications contain code snippets that are repeatedly executed with fixed workload, which can be used for performance variance detection. To effectively identify these snippets at runtime even without program source code, we introduce State Transition Graph (STG) to track program execution and then conduct lightweight workload analysis on STG to locate variance. To diagnose the detected variance, Vapro leverages a progressive diagnosis method based on a hybrid model leveraging variance breakdown and statistical analysis. Results show that the performance overhead of Vapro is only 1.38% on average. Vapro can detect the variance in real applications caused by hardware bugs, memory, and IO. After fixing the detected variance, the standard deviation of the execution time is reduced by up to 73.5%. Compared with the state-of-the-art variance detection tool based on source code analysis, Vapro achieves 30.0% higher detection coverage."},"title":{"value":"Vapro: performance variance detection and diagnosis for production-run parallel applications"},"authors":{"value":["Liyan Zheng","Jidong Zhai","Xiongchao Tang","Haojie Wang","Teng Yu","Yuyang Jin","Shuaiwen Leon Song","Wenguang Chen"]}},"tmdate":1762325506097,"pdate":1640995200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/ppopp/ZhengZTWYJSC22"],"tcdate":1762325469270,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jidong_Zhai1"],"forum":"T4Xzki6Xgx","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":656920,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762325506097,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"T4Xzki6Xgx","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2310.07854v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"hsiao|vapr_variableprecision_tensors_to_accelerate_robot_motion_planning"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yu-Shun_Hsiao:","~Siva_Kumar_Sastry_Hari1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Balakumar_Sundaralingam:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jason_Yik:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Thierry_Tambe:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Charbel_Sakr:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Stephen_W._Keckler:","~Vijay_Janapa_Reddi1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2310.07854"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2310-07854,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yu-Shun Hsiao and Siva Kumar Sastry Hari and Balakumar Sundaralingam and Jason Yik and Thierry Tambe and Charbel Sakr and Stephen W. Keckler and Vijay Janapa Reddi},\n  title={VaPr: Variable-Precision Tensors to Accelerate Robot Motion Planning},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2310.07854},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2310.07854}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"High-dimensional motion generation requires numerical precision for smooth, collision-free solutions. Typically, double-precision or single-precision floating-point (FP) formats are utilized. Using these for big tensors imposes a strain on the memory bandwidth provided by the devices and alters the memory footprint, hence limiting their applicability to low-power edge devices needed for mobile robots. The uniform application of reduced precision can be advantageous but severely degrades solutions. Using decreased precision data types for important tensors, we propose to accelerate motion generation by removing memory bottlenecks. We propose variable-precision (VaPr) search optimization to determine the appropriate precision for large tensors from a vast search space of approximately 4 million unique combinations for FP data types across the tensors. To obtain the efficiency gains, we exploit existing platform support for an out-of-the-box GPU speedup and evaluate prospective precision converter units for GPU types that are not currently supported. Our experimental results on 800 planning problems for the Franka Panda robot on the MotionBenchmaker dataset across 8 environments show that a 4-bit FP format is sufficient for the largest set of tensors in the motion generation stack. With the software-only solution, VaPr achieves 6.3% and 6.3% speedups on average for a significant portion of motion generation over the SOTA solution (CuRobo) on Jetson Orin and RTX2080 Ti GPU, respectively, and 9.9%, 17.7% speedups with the FP converter."},"title":{"value":"VaPr: Variable-Precision Tensors to Accelerate Robot Motion Planning"},"authors":{"value":["Yu-Shun Hsiao","Siva Kumar Sastry Hari","Balakumar Sundaralingam","Jason Yik","Thierry Tambe","Charbel Sakr","Stephen W. Keckler","Vijay Janapa Reddi"]}},"tmdate":1778194420994,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1731504591443,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Vijay_Janapa_Reddi1"],"forum":"XjcxqUB7o9","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":227806,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1778194420994,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"XjcxqUB7o9","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IROS 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10341341/10341342/10342109.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IROS/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"hsiao|vapr_variableprecision_tensors_to_accelerate_robot_motion_planning"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yu-Shun_Hsiao:","~Siva_Kumar_Sastry_Hari1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Balakumar_Sundaralingam:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jason_Yik:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Thierry_Tambe:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Charbel_Sakr:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Stephen_W._Keckler:","~Vijay_Janapa_Reddi1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS55552.2023.10342109"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iros/HsiaoHSYTSKR23,\n  author={Yu-Shun Hsiao and Siva Kumar Sastry Hari and Balakumar Sundaralingam and Jason Yik and Thierry Tambe and Charbel Sakr and Stephen W. Keckler and Vijay Janapa Reddi},\n  title={VaPr: Variable-Precision Tensors to Accelerate Robot Motion Planning},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={6304-6309},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS55552.2023.10342109},\n  booktitle={IROS}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"High-dimensional motion generation requires nu-merical precision for smooth, collision-free solutions. Typically, double-precision or single-precision floating-point (FP) formats are utilized. Using these for big tensors imposes a strain on the memory bandwidth provided by the devices and alters the memory footprint, hence limiting their applicability to low-power edge devices needed for mobile robots. The uniform application of reduced precision can be advantageous but severely degrades solutions. Using decreased precision data types for important tensors, we propose to accelerate motion generation by removing memory bottlenecks. We propose variable-precision (VaPr) search optimization to determine the appropriate precision for large tensors from a vast search space of approximately 4 million unique combinations for FP data types across the tensors. To obtain the efficiency gains, we exploit existing platform support for an out-of-the-box GPU speedup and evaluate prospective precision converter units for GPU types that are not currently supported. Our experimental results on 800 planning problems for the Franka Panda robot on the MotionBenchmaker dataset across 8 environments show that a 4-bit FP format is sufficient for the largest set of tensors in the motion generation stack. With the software-only solution, VaPr achieves 6.3% and 6.3% speedups on average for a significant portion of motion generation over the SOTA solution (CuRobo) on Jetson Orin and RTX2080 Ti GPU, respectively, and 9.9%, 17.7% speedups with the FP converter. More details are available at sites.google.com/nvidia.com/vapr."},"title":{"value":"VaPr: Variable-Precision Tensors to Accelerate Robot Motion Planning"},"authors":{"value":["Yu-Shun Hsiao","Siva Kumar Sastry Hari","Balakumar Sundaralingam","Jason Yik","Thierry Tambe","Charbel Sakr","Stephen W. Keckler","Vijay Janapa Reddi"]}},"tmdate":1778194420973,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1731504592232,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Vijay_Janapa_Reddi1"],"forum":"TEOqdFfLMJ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":227854,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1778194420973,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"TEOqdFfLMJ","version":2}],"count":5}